{:edb/db-before-t 1863 :edb/db-after-t 1864 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535176 50 #inst "2026-10-08T18:45:10.086Z" 13194139535176 true] [17592186114989 1100 "Is\n$E = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nan elementary matrix?" 13194139535176 false] [17592186114989 1100 "Is \n$\\begin{aligned}E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$ \nan elementary matrix?" 13194139535176 true] [17592186114989 1145 "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation.\n\nThe given matrix $E$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I_2$ by applying the elementary row operation\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2+(-5) R_1\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{2} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + (-5)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= E\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535176 false] [17592186114989 1145 "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation.\n\nThe given matrix $E$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I_2$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_2:= R_2+(-5) R_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535176 true] [17592186114990 1100 "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535176 false] [17592186114990 1100 "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/65e27c97ac04ff5514c6e1be3b010e7a6efa464131bfbad03a569f85e728ac85.png)" 13194139535176 true] [17592186114990 1145 "We write down $E$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}E & I\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=\\frac{5}{2}R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535176 false] [17592186114990 1145 "We write down $E$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/cd8839007e548fcd0858ebc42904d3659d639da834b8caa476c4765b5b1a6b69.png)" 13194139535176 true] [17592186114991 1100 "Find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1}$, where $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535176 false] [17592186114991 1100 "Find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1},$ where ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/ad2295d047cfcd60a05c2b155e8c21c511d295e76b0ad4056cf136fe54c272a1.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/299ce95f91a5eb4029b2a8512c0b45c24562fac91cc4c70a7e00ecf42c320099.png)" 13194139535176 true] [17592186114991 1145 "Notice that $E_1$ and $E_2$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find $E_2^{-1}$. We write down $E_2$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}E_{2} & I\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1}:=R_{1} + 2R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, we can find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1}$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535176 false] [17592186114991 1145 "Notice that $E_1$ and $E_2$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find $E_2^{-1}.$ We write down $E_2$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/a8c0d76a5f06d1077eb45b6af4761434164602747f54d9644646b654d319b373.png)\n\nNow, we can find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/f16a0b0e573cf3a8022f9c62993364a0d708c3a0cff0b4bb4cb0eadde7848f56.png)" 13194139535176 true] [17592186115163 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335559 13194139535176 true] [17592186115163 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335566 13194139535176 true] [17592186115163 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335573 13194139535176 true] [17592186115163 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335615 13194139535176 true] [17592186115163 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335622 13194139535176 true] [17592186115164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335580 13194139535176 true] [17592186115164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335587 13194139535176 true] [17592186115164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335594 13194139535176 true] [17592186115164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335629 13194139535176 true] [17592186115164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335636 13194139535176 true] [17592186115165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335601 13194139535176 true] [17592186115165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335608 13194139535176 true] [17592186115165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335643 13194139535176 true] [17592186115165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335650 13194139535176 true] [17592186115165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335657 13194139535176 true] [17592186335559 1017 #edb/ref 17592186335560 13194139535176 true] [17592186335559 1089 "q-130772" 13194139535176 true] [17592186335559 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535176 true] [17592186335559 1100 "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535176 true] [17592186335559 1113 #uuid "0d49dd3c-2d26-5daf-baa6-13e98e158d10" 13194139535176 true] [17592186335559 1145 "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $P$ and $Q$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $S$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =S\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $S$ is an elementary matrix." 13194139535176 true] [1759218